      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's distinctive visual language, crafted by cinematographer Barry Sonnenfeld, creates a live-action Looney Tunes feel through extreme angles, whip pans, and elaborate tracking shots. This hyperactive style influenced countless films, from <a href=\"/movie/241\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"241\">Natural Born Killers</a> (1994) to <a href=\"/movie/22538\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"22538\">Scott Pilgrim vs. the World</a> (2010). The Coens blend slapstick comedy with darker themes about parenthood, redemption, and the American Dream gone sideways - a combination they'd perfect in later films like <a href=\"/movie/275\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"275\">Fargo</a> (1996) and <a href=\"/movie/134\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"134\">O Brother, Where Art Thou?</a> (2000)."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Nicolas Cage delivers one of his most perfectly calibrated performances, balancing wild-eyed intensity with genuine pathos. His chemistry with Holly Hunter creates an oddly touching love story amid the mayhem. The supporting cast, including John Goodman and William Forsythe as escaped convicts, and Randall \"Tex\" Cobb as the demonic biker Leonard Smalls, exemplifies the Coens' knack for memorable character actors that would become a hallmark in films like <a href=\"/movie/115\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"115\">The Big Lebowski</a> (1998) and <a href=\"/movie/6977\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"6977\">No Country for Old Men</a> (2007). SUBHEAD: Comedy Meets Crime"
      },
